# The second layer's bounce: the guest throws the crate at the wall and it falls back into six
# light boxes the host owns. With the layer the boxes answer in the step the crate reaches them;
# without it they stand until the host's answer plays (one way, the host's delay, one way, this
# guest's delay: about 16 ticks here). Bounds, derived: response_ticks.max 2 (the copy moves in
# the contact's own step; one tick for a contact that begins at a step's end; one more step when a
# resting copy is met from standstill); rest at 1 cm and 2 degrees, no rest wakes, no rest jitter.
scenario layer_bounce
scene wall_boxes
seed 51
guests 1
# A hard end well past rest, so the rest measures watch the world at rest for seconds.
end 14s
light_mass 20kg

net g1 rtt 100ms jitter 20ms loss 2%

p1 walk crate
p1 grab crate as grab
p1 wait 300ms
p1 throw wall speed 12 as throw

expect result grab granted
expect result throw done
# Derived: the copy answers in its own step (0), or one later when the hit lands at the step's
# end (1), plus one step when it rested in the hold and the body met it from standstill (a wall
# for one step: README "The second layer"): 2.
expect response_ticks.max <= 2t
expect agree_at_rest 1cm 2deg
expect no_stuck
expect rest_wakes.count <= 0
# Once at rest after the last action, a guest's world stays at rest: 0.
expect rest_breaks.count <= 0
expect response_misses.count <= 0
# Derived: a copy moves on while its owner rests for at most the longest pause (60 ticks, a hit
# among soft copies), then the owner's stillness (15), the playback delay (at most 15) and the
# one-way delay (8 at 250 ms, one more a tick per extra ~33 ms); in the rest hold it stops at
# once. 60 + 15 + 15 + 8 = 98.
expect rest_lag_ticks.max <= 98t
# Derived: per episode of this side's contact a copy may take one snap (past 1 m), one stalled
# pull (30 ticks unpressed) and one rest-hold entry off the dead zone: 3. Episodes here: 1
# (the crate's hit). 3 x 1 = 3.
expect soft_places.max <= 3
expect rest_jitter.max <= 0m
# Derived: a copy's visible correction in a tick (its drawn move less its owner's at the same
# event time, or less its own step for a predicted copy) is at most a render offset's release,
# 0.15 of at most 2 m: 0.3 m. A placement the offset does not take shows as its whole move.
expect jump.max <= 0.3m
# Derived: a body this guest simulates is moved by the seam only when it becomes local, and the
# render offset takes that move under either policy (a take by interaction keeps its pose): float
# noise, 1 cm.
expect local_correction.max <= 1cm
